sentences: what breaks, and for whom. GOOD: The recommend-level script prints a 17-line report, so a caller that needs only the level cannot parse the answer. WEAK: The script has issues with output. -->\n\n### Problem Statement\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What is broken, missing, or inefficient? 2-3 sentences describing the specific pain point.]\n\n### Purpose\n[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n<!-- FILL: List three to five things this change does, each starting with a verb. GOOD: Add a `--quiet` flag that prints only the recommended-level line. WEAK: Update the script and docs. -->\n\n### In Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 1]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 2]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Deliverable 3]\n\n### Out of Scope\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 1] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Excluded item 2] - [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: why]\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: path/to/file.js] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Modify/Create/Delete] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Brief description] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Write one testable requirement per row. Name the exact command or output. GOOD: With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line that starts with `Recommended Level:`. WEAK: The flag should work well. -->\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-002 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Requirement description] |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n<!-- FILL: Write one measurable outcome per line. A command can prove it. GOOD: `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 500 --files 15` prints one line and exits 0. WEAK: The feature is successful. -->\n\n- **SC-001**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Primary measurable outcome]\n- **SC-002**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Secondary measurable outcome]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n<!-- FILL: Name one dependency and one risk. Give each a concrete mitigation. GOOD: A caller parses the level line and breaks when the label changes. Mitigation: keep the label stable and test it. WEAK: There may be some risk. -->\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: System/API] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: What if blocked] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback plan] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk description] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: High/Med/Low] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Mitigation strategy] |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Name one measurable limit per line. Write `N/A` and one reason when the packet runs no service, or delete the whole section. GOOD: The flag adds no measurable time: the same 40 ms run. WEAK: Performance is good. -->\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Response time target - e.g., <200ms p95]\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth requirement - e.g., JWT tokens required]\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Uptime target - e.g., 99.9%]\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n<!-- FILL: Name the empty input, the maximum input and the failure path. One line each. GOOD: Empty input: the script exits 2 with usage. Maximum: 10,000 files scores in under one second. WEAK: Handle edge cases. -->\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: How system handles]\n- Maximum length: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Limit and behavior]\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Fallback behavior]\n- Network timeout: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Retry strategy]\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Recovery behavior]\n- Session expiry: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: User experience]\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n<!-- FILL: Score each row with the number the change earns. The total must match the level. GOOD: Scope 4/25: one file and 12 lines. Total 18/100. WEAK: Complexity is medium. -->\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Files: X, LOC: Y, Systems: Z] |\n| Risk | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /25] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Auth: Y/N, API: Y/N, Breaking: Y/N] |\n| Research | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /20] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Investigation needs] |\n| Multi-Agent | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Workstreams: X] |\n| Coordination | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /15] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Dependencies: X] |\n| **Total** | **[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: /100]** | **Level 3** |\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n<!-- FILL: List each risk with impact, likelihood and one mitigation. GOOD: R1: a caller parses the label. Impact M, likelihood L. Mitigation: a test pins the label. WEAK: Some risks exist. -->\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Risk] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: H/M/L] | [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Strategy] |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n<!-- FILL: Write one story per user. Name the actor, the want and the benefit. GOOD: As a build script, I want one parseable line, so that I can skip the report. WEAK: Users want a quiet mode. -->\n\n### US-001: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Title] (Priority: P1)\n\n**As a** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: user type], **I want** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: needed behavior], **so that** [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: benefit].\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n<!-- FILL: Write each open question as one line. Write `None` when nothing blocks. GOOD: Does `--quiet` suppress warnings? No, stderr is unchanged. WEAK: Edge cases are unclear. -->\n\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 1 requiring clarification]\n- [YOUR_VALUE_HERE: Question 2 requiring clarification]\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n<!-- FILL: Leave the four links in place. -->\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `decision-record.md`\n\n---\n\n\n","newText":"---\ntitle: \"Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\"\ndescription: \"Callers that need only the recommended level must parse a 17-line human report or decode JSON. A --quiet flag prints the single Recommended Level line while every existing invocation stays byte-identical.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"recommend level quiet flag\"\n  - \"add a quiet flag to recommend level sh\"\nimportance_tier: \"normal\"\ncontextType: \"general\"\n---\n<!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: spec-core + level2-verify + level3-arch | v2.3 -->\n# Feature Specification: Add a quiet flag to recommend-level.sh\n\n<!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 3 -->\n\n---\n\n## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\n\n`recommend-level.sh` answers one question, but its human-readable answer is a 17-line report: the recommended level is the second line, followed by the score, a six-row breakdown and the phase section. A caller that needs only the level has to parse the report or switch to `--json` and decode it. This packet adds a `--quiet` flag that prints only the `Recommended Level:` line, keeps the default report byte-identical, and is proved by a baseline diff plus a one-line count.\n\n**Key Decisions**: `--quiet` is an opt-in flag, not an environment variable; `--json` keeps precedence when both flags are passed; both output paths render the level line through one shared helper.\n\n**Critical Dependencies**: `bash` 3.2 or newer, the existing `output_text` report format, and `node` for the phase-system regression suite. No new package.\n\n---\n<!-- ANCHOR:metadata -->\n## 1. METADATA\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Level** | 3 |\n| **Priority** | P1 |\n| **Status** | Draft |\n| **Created** | 2026-10-25 |\n| **Branch** | `worktrees/224-deepseek-final2` |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:metadata -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:problem -->\n## 2. PROBLEM & PURPOSE\n\n### Problem Statement\n\n`recommend-level.sh` reports a single recommended level inside a 17-line human report. A build script, CI step or agent prompt that needs only the level must either parse that report or invoke `--json` and run a JSON decoder, and both routes cost more than reading one line. There is no output mode between the full report and a structured document.\n\n### Purpose\n\nGive callers a one-line, stable, greppable answer with `--quiet`, without changing what any existing invocation prints.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:problem -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:scope -->\n## 3. SCOPE\n\n### In Scope\n- Add a `--quiet` flag, with `-q` as its short alias, to the argument parser.\n- Add a single-line output path that prints `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)` and nothing else.\n- Document the flag in the script header OPTIONS block and in `show_help`.\n- Prove the default report is byte-identical and the quiet output is exactly one line.\n\n### Out of Scope\n- Changing scoring, thresholds or phase logic - the request is an output mode, not a behavior change.\n- Suppressing stderr - errors must stay visible when stdout is quiet.\n- Changing the `--json` document - it is already a compact machine format and its consumers are unaffected.\n- Adding the same flag to other scripts - `create.sh`, `validate.sh` and the rest keep their current output.\n\n### Files to Change\n\n| File Path | Change Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh` | Modify | Add the flag, the shared level-line helper, the single-line output path, and the two documentation blocks that describe the flag. |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:scope -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:requirements -->\n## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n\n### P0 - Blockers (MUST complete)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-001 | With `--quiet`, stdout holds exactly one line, `Recommended Level: <N> (<Name>)`, and the exit code is 0. |\n| REQ-002 | Without `--quiet`, stdout is byte-identical to the pre-change output for the same arguments, for both the simple and the complex fixture. |\n| REQ-003 | `--quiet` appears in the script header OPTIONS block and in `--help` output with a one-line description. |\n\n### P1 - Required (complete OR user-approved deferral)\n\n| ID | Requirement |\n|----|-------------|\n| REQ-004 | `-q` is accepted as an alias for `--quiet` and produces identical output. |\n| REQ-005 | When `--quiet` and `--json` are both passed, the JSON document wins and stdout remains one valid JSON document. |\n| REQ-006 | Invalid input with `--quiet` still writes its error to stderr, prints nothing to stdout, and exits 1. |\n\n> Acceptance criteria for these requirements live in `acceptance-criteria.md`,\n> which is the document that decides whether this packet may close.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:requirements -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n## 5. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n\n- **SC-001**: `recommend-level.sh --quiet --loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural` prints exactly one line and exits 0.\n- **SC-002**: The same invocation without `--quiet` produces a diff of zero bytes against the pre-change baseline.\n- **SC-003**: `recommend-level.sh --help` lists `--quiet` and `-q`.\n- **SC-004**: `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-phase-system.js` still prints `passed=27 failed=0`.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:success-criteria -->\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:risks -->\n## 6. RISKS & DEPENDENCIES\n\n| Type | Item | Impact | Mitigation |\n|------|------|--------|------------|\n| Dependency | `bash` 3.2 or newer, the version macOS ships | The script cannot run at all | Use only features the script already uses; no new builtins or arrays beyond what exists. |\n| Dependency | `node` for the phase-system regression suite | SC-004 cannot be checked | The suite is a verification aid, not a runtime dependency; the shell assertions still prove the contract. |\n| Risk | A caller parses the quiet line and breaks if the label changes | Medium | Reuse the exact report line through one shared helper and pin it in acceptance criterion AC-001. |\n| Risk | The default report drifts by a blank line or spacing | High | Capture baselines before the edit and diff them after; a non-empty diff blocks completion. |\n| Risk | `--quiet --json` becomes ambiguous | Low | JSON wins by decision record ADR-002 and is covered by AC-006. |\n<!-- /ANCHOR:risks -->\n\n---\n\n\n## 7. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n### Performance\n- **NFR-P01**: The quiet path adds no subshell and no extra process, so a quiet invocation stays within the measured baseline of 0.01 seconds `real` on the reference machine (`/usr/bin/time -p`).\n\n### Security\n- **NFR-S01**: N/A - the script reads no credential and touches no file; `--quiet` changes stdout formatting only.\n\n### Reliability\n- **NFR-R01**: Exit codes are unchanged: 0 on success and 1 on invalid input, with or without `--quiet`.\n\n---\n\n## 8. EDGE CASES\n\n### Data Boundaries\n- Empty input: `--quiet` alone (no `--loc`, no `--files`) exits 1 with `ERROR: At least --loc or --files must be provided` on stderr and nothing on stdout.\n- Maximum input: `--loc 5000 --files 50` caps at the weight totals; the quiet path still prints one line with `Recommended Level: 3 (Full)`.\n\n### Error Scenarios\n- External service failure: N/A - the script makes no network call.\n- Network timeout: N/A - the script makes no network call.\n- Unknown option: `--quiet --bogus` exits 1 with `ERROR: Unknown option: --bogus` on stderr.\n\n### State Transitions\n- Partial completion: N/A - the script computes once and writes no state.\n- Session expiry: N/A - the script is stateless per invocation.\n- Repeated flag: `--quiet --quiet` is idempotent and still prints one line.\n- Flag position: `--quiet` before, between or after the other flags yields the same output because parsing is order-independent.\n\n---\n\n## 9. COMPLEXITY ASSESSMENT\n\n| Dimension | Score | Triggers |\n|-----------|-------|----------|\n| Scope | 6/25 | Files: 1, LOC: ~20, Systems: 1 script |\n| Risk | 8/25 | Auth: N, API: N, Breaking: N; output-contract compatibility is the only risk |\n| Research | 4/20 | Baseline line counts and flag-precedence semantics must be established before the edit |\n| Multi-Agent | 0/15 | Workstreams: 1 |\n| Coordination | 2/15 | Dependencies: 1 (script, docs and regression suite must agree) |\n| **Total** | **20/100** | **Level 3** |\n\nThe change-intrinsic score is 20/100, which sits in the Level 1 band. This packet is authored at Level 3 because the `zz-bench-authoring` track fixes that level; the higher level adds documentation and verification depth, not code surface.\n\n---\n\n## 10. RISK MATRIX\n\n| Risk ID | Description | Impact | Likelihood | Mitigation |\n|---------|-------------|--------|------------|------------|\n| R-001 | The default report drifts by one byte or one blank line | H | L | Capture both baselines before the edit and diff them in AC-002 and AC-003. |\n| R-002 | The quiet line diverges from the report line over time | M | L | Render both through one helper (`_level_line`) and assert equality in AC-001. |\n| R-003 | `--quiet --json` produces a half-format that no consumer can parse | L | M | JSON precedence in ADR-002, verified by AC-006. |\n| R-004 | The help text and the header OPTIONS block disagree | L | M | Update both in one task and check both in AC-004. |\n\n---\n\n## 11. USER STORIES\n\n### US-001: One-line level for scripts (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** build script or CI step, **I want** the recommended level on a single line, **so that** I can read it without parsing a report or decoding JSON.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-002: Unchanged default report (Priority: P0)\n\n**As a** maintainer of an existing caller, **I want** the default report to stay byte-identical, **so that** my current parsing and snapshots keep working.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n### US-003: Discoverable flag (Priority: P1)\n\n**As an** operator reading `--help`, **I want** `--quiet` listed with its effect, **so that** I can find the mode without reading the script.\n\n**Acceptance criteria:** see `acceptance-criteria.md` (rows referencing this story).\n\n---\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:questions -->\n## 12. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\nNone. Two semantics that could have been questions are decided instead: `--quiet` does not touch stderr (errors stay visible), and `--json` wins when both flags are passed (ADR-002 in `plan.md`).\n<!-- /ANCHOR:questions -->\n\n---\n\n## RELATED DOCUMENTS\n\n- **Implementation Plan**: See `plan.md`\n- **Task Breakdown**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Verification Checklist**: See `tasks.md`\n- **Decision Records**: See `plan.md`, section \"L3: ARCHITECTURE DECISION RECORD\"\n\n---\n","_meta":{"cognition.ai/linesAdded":72,"cognition.ai/linesRemoved":60}}],"rawInput":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/224-deepseek-final2/specs/zz-bench-authoring/001-recommend-level-quiet-flag/spec.md"},"_meta":{"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"write"}}
